WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.020
Can priority scheduling starve a

2
00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.720
process? Yes: a ready process

3
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.940
can wait indefinitely if more

4
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.880
highly ranked work always wins

5
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.600
selection. Priority decides whom to

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.180
choose, while preemption decides whether

7
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.840
the current execution can be

8
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.160
interrupted; they are separate policy

9
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:19.660
choices.

10
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.020
A priority number is meaningful

11
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.240
only with its direction specified.

12
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.440
The slides commonly use a

13
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.520
smaller integer for higher priority.

14
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.420
A system using the opposite

15
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.820
convention would choose differently, so

16
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.500
write the convention before tracing

17
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:34.960
anything.

18
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.560
Imagine low-priority process L is

19
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.160
ready at zero and needs

20
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.120
two processor units. A high-priority

21
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.080
process H is also ready

22
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.840
and can remain runnable forever.

23
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.420
If selection always favors H,

24
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.860
every new scheduling decision selects

25
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:52.500
H again.

26
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.720
Watch timer ticks repeatedly preempt

27
00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.280
the current execution and create

28
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.540
new choices. Nothing in strict

29
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.480
priority requires those choices to

30
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.540
alternate between classes. If H

31
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.460
stays eligible, L can remain

32
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.020
ready without ever receiving its

33
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.060
first processor unit.

34
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.480
That is exactly the conceptual

35
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.760
risk in the lab strict

36
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.700
even-identifier policy on one processor.

37
00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.480
An eligible even-identifier process must

38
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.660
win over odd-identifier work. An

39
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.700
odd process can therefore starve

40
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.240
if the favored class never

41
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.980
stops providing runnable work.

42
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.240
Finite high-priority work produces postponement,

43
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.920
but not necessarily indefinite starvation.

44
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.300
If H eventually blocks or

45
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.500
finishes and no other high-priority

46
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.720
process is eligible, L can

47
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.660
run. A short observation of

48
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.180
no output does not prove

49
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.200
an infinite execution history by

50
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:46.600
itself.

51
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.140
Aging changes the selection rule

52
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.980
by improving the effective priority

53
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.300
of a waiting ready process.

54
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.800
In this authored illustration, L

55
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.320
is promoted to outrank H

56
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.380
after six waiting units, so

57
00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.640
the next selection can give

58
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.360
L the processor. A real

59
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.480
guarantee depends on bounded priority

60
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.040
levels and the complete tie

61
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.000
and selection rules.

62
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.460
Distinguish this from a deadlocked

63
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.940
process waiting for an event

64
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.080
it cannot cause. A ready

65
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.900
starving process is eligible but

66
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.580
repeatedly unselected; a blocked process

67
00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.740
may not be a scheduling

68
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.800
candidate at all. Raising the

69
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.540
priority of a blocked process

70
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.320
does not necessarily release the

71
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:23.880
lock it needs.

72
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.000
On multiple processors, one favored

73
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.700
process cannot execute simultaneously on

74
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.520
every core in a sequential-process

75
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.240
model. Therefore a one-core starvation

76
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.920
example must not be copied

77
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.280
unchanged as a proof for

78
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.560
several cores. Check whether enough

79
00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.480
favored runnable work exists at

80
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.200
each selection.

81
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.960
Things to take note of.

82
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.100
State priority direction, preemptive behavior,

83
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.700
ties and the workload that

84
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.140
keeps preferred work eligible. Use

85
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.860
aging to explain a changed

86
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.520
fairness rule, and never claim

87
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:55.960
that a timer interrupt by

88
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.920
itself guarantees that every ready

89
00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.260
process gets a turn.
